[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Behavioral Patterns: Observer and Strategy"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Behavioral design patterns are concerned with algorithms and the assignment of responsibilities between objects. They describe not just patterns of objects or classes but also the patterns of communication between them. This tutorial will cover two important behavioral patterns: Observer and Strategy."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Observer Pattern"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified and updated automatically. It's also known as Publish-Subscribe."},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Key components:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Subject: The object whose state changes. It maintains a list of observers and provides methods to attach and detach observers."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Observer: An interface or abstract class that defines the update method, which is called when the subject's state changes."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concrete Observers: Implement the Observer interface. Each concrete observer registers with a subject to receive updates."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Observer Pattern Example (Python)"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"class Subject:
    def __init__(self):
        self._observers = []
        self._state = None

    def attach(self, observer):
        self._observers.append(observer)

    def detach(self, observer):
        self._observers.remove(observer)

    def notify(self):
        for observer in self._observers:
            observer.update(self._state)

    def set_state(self, state):
        self._state = state
        self.notify()

class Observer:
    def update(self, state):
        pass

class ConcreteObserverA(Observer):
    def update(self, state):
        print(f'ConcreteObserverA: State changed to {state}')

class ConcreteObserverB(Observer):
    def update(self, state):
        print(f'ConcreteObserverB: State changed to {state}')

# Usage
subject = Subject()
observer_a = ConcreteObserverA()
observer_b = ConcreteObserverB()

subject.attach(observer_a)
subject.attach(observer_b)

subject.set_state(10)
subject.detach(observer_a)
subject.set_state(20)"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Strategy Pattern"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Strategy pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. Strategy lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it. It promotes the 'Open/Closed Principle' because you can add new strategies without modifying the classes that use them."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Key components:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strategy: An interface or abstract class that defines the algorithm."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concrete Strategies: Implement the Strategy interface. Each concrete strategy provides a different implementation of the algorithm."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Context: A class that maintains a reference to a Strategy object. The context is not aware of the concrete strategy being used. It delegates the algorithm execution to the strategy."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Strategy Pattern Example (Python)"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"class Strategy:
    def execute(self, data):
        pass

class ConcreteStrategyA(Strategy):
    def execute(self, data):
        return sorted(data)

class ConcreteStrategyB(Strategy):
    def execute(self, data):
        return reversed(sorted(data))

class Context:
    def __init__(self, strategy):
        self._strategy = strategy

    def set_strategy(self, strategy):
        self._strategy = strategy

    def execute_strategy(self, data):
        return self._strategy.execute(data)

# Usage
data = [1, 5, 2, 4, 3]

context = Context(ConcreteStrategyA())
result_a = context.execute_strategy(data)
print(f'Result A: {result_a}')

context.set_strategy(ConcreteStrategyB())
result_b = context.execute_strategy(data)
print(f'Result B: {result_b}')"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Observer and Strategy patterns are powerful tools for designing flexible and maintainable software. The Observer pattern allows for loose coupling between objects, while the Strategy pattern enables you to easily switch between different algorithms at runtime. Understanding and applying these patterns can lead to more robust and adaptable applications."}]
